1406264}},{"text":"If ￼ is a permutation matrix, it directly encodes a map ￼","targetRectangle":{"y":297,"x":173.49999956130978,"width":1171.541097482007,"height":100.759375136453}},{"text":"For dense couplings, we can still define a map via the barycentric projection:        ￼      (for general measures, ￼)","targetRectangle":{"y":398.74049085910923,"x":173.49999956130978,"width":1308.393599999999,"height":161.5124911812913}},{"text":"In matrix form, for data ￼ and ￼, we can write ￼","targetRectangle":{"y":561.23409776305675,"x":173.49999956130978,"width":1352.7538107327105,"height":93.184227357013697}},{"text":"Probabilistic view: ￼, i.e., conditional expectation under ￼","targetRectangle":{"y":655.39944084272679,"x":173.49999956130978,"width":1260.403377322916,"height":95.07960154019122}},{"text":"Usefulness: gives an approximate Monge map for tasks like domain adaptation, generative modeling, or style transfer.","targetRectangle":{"y":751.49544619151175,"x":173.49999956130978,"width":1467.15984,"height":126.08888540267947}},{"text":"Limitation: only defined on the training\/source samples ￼; no direct out-of-sample extension.","targetRectangle":{"y":878.56544731684744,"x":173.49999956130978,"width":1505.6476335476627,"height":94.869239555144532}},{"text":"equation.pdf","targetRectangle":{"y":340.07000112533569,"x":202.68831956130978,"width":21.332482990654199,"height":49.309919564790789}},{"text":"equation.pdf","targetRectangle":{"y":484.81049198444487,"x":896.67819801410815,"width":487.9882641965437,"height":75.442490055955545}},{"text":"equation.pdf","targetRectangle":{"y":698.46944196806248,"x":1412.5708938935716,"width":21.332482990654171,"height":49.309919564790789}},{"text":"equation.pdf","targetRectangle":{"y":264.40783798694611,"x":1818.9216918945312,"width":33.813389637540922,"height":32.998798033118248}},{"text":"equation.pdf","targetRectangle":{"y":361.59999847412109,"x":1817.3509826660156,"width":69.199995646476737,"height":40.719997572898876}},{"tex